# OpenTofu / Terraform.
# State is the sensitive one: harness_platform_secret_text stores the secret
# VALUE in plaintext in state, so the GitHub App private key lives there in
# base64. Never commit it, and treat the state file like the .pem itself.
terraform/.terraform/
terraform/*.tfstate
terraform/*.tfstate.*
terraform/*.tfvars
!terraform/*.tfvars.example
crash.log

# THE SAME RULES AT ANY DEPTH.
# The patterns above are anchored one level under terraform/, so they cover
# terraform/ itself and NOTHING under terraform/<workspace>/. A per-workspace
# layout therefore slipped straight past them: `tofu init` in
# terraform/test-fleet/ vendored the AWS provider and an 814 MB
# terraform-provider-aws binary was committed. Pushed history has no blob over
# 500 KB, so that was the first time - and once such a blob is pushed it is in
# history permanently and every clone pays for it.
**/.terraform/
**/*.tfstate
**/*.tfstate.*
**/*.tfstate.backup

# Saved plans. Binary, short-lived, and they embed the full resource config
# including anything the run resolved.
**/tfplan
**/*.tfplan

# .terraform.lock.hcl is deliberately NOT ignored - it pins provider versions
# and belongs in the repo. Only the vendored provider BINARIES under
# .terraform/ are the problem.

# Per-workspace *.tfvars stay tracked on purpose (terraform/eks-hub/hub.auto.tfvars
# is real configuration, and the secret it needs comes from TF_VAR_harness_api_token
# in the environment, never from the file).

# Never the key material itself, wherever it lands.
*.pem
*.key

# NOTE: charts/*/charts/*.tgz and Chart.lock are committed ON PURPOSE - the
# gitops-helm dependency is vendored so nothing is fetched from the internet
# at deploy time, which is the usual reason Native Helm fails on a
# locked-down cluster.
